# SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-only
# THE ONE PLACE THE HEADLESS BROWSER IS PINNED (#254, #447).
#
# Sourced by every suite that drives a real Chromium:
#   scripts/hostile-content-e2e.sh   nothing executes, nothing is fetched
#   scripts/inline-images-e2e.sh     cid: renders, remote stays blocked
#   scripts/a11y-e2e.sh              axe + the accessibility tree (#447)
#   scripts/proxied-images-e2e.sh    proxied images render behind a real login (#667)
#
# It is a file rather than three copies of one string because the copies already
# had an invariant written on them in prose — "keep the two scripts on the SAME
# digest; they are testing the same renderer, and a split would make one suite's
# probe say nothing about the other's" — and a third suite is where prose stops
# being enough. A shared file makes that structural.
#
# PINNED BY DIGEST, and the reasoning is the two older suites', unchanged: every
# assertion they make is a NEGATIVE one (nothing executed, nothing was fetched,
# the sandbox held). Negative assertions are exactly the kind that start passing
# for the wrong reason when the thing underneath them changes — a sandbox
# attribute that becomes a no-op, a CSP directive that is newly ignored, a frame
# that no longer loads at all. A green run would look identical, and on a
# floating tag it would arrive with no commit, no review and no changelog entry.
# That is the same failure mode as #131, where three assertions about a sandbox
# passed against a page that could not have loaded.
#
# #447's lane adds a POSITIVE reason for the same pin: a computed accessible
# name, a computed contrast ratio and an accessibility tree are all the BROWSER's
# answers, not ours. A browser bump can change every number this lane prints
# without a line of Mailroom changing, and a required check whose verdict depends
# on when it ran is not a gate.
#
# This digest carries:  Chromium 124.0.6367.78 / puppeteer 22.9.0 / node v20.15.1
#
# ⚠ ON A DELIBERATE BUMP, re-run EVERY suite above AND their probes —
# hostile-content's reporter probe, #131's sandbox controls, a11y-e2e's three
# negative controls, and proxied-images' in-frame control. A browser change is precisely when "does this test still fail
# when it should?" stops being a formality.
#
# The env override is kept so a candidate image can be tried without editing this
# line. It is a POSIX `sh` assignment and nothing else: this file is sourced by
# `set -eu` scripts, so an unset CHROME_IMAGE afterwards is an immediate, loud
# failure rather than a suite quietly running against the local default.
CHROME_IMAGE="${CHROME_IMAGE:-zenika/alpine-chrome:with-puppeteer@sha256:ee10e24217aa27443e6b58da628f3b09ea9b814459915b8b62fe15a555f9692a}"
